    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d858">

                <pb facs="0915" n="903" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg903"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00118 European War 1939/446: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-07-26T11:00:00+03:00">July 26, 1941—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 27—11:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1411. In a conversation with Lozovski Friday<anchor n="43" xml:id="d858-f1"/> he
                    stated that while a decision as to the stationing of American naval observers in
                    Vladivostok had not yet been arrived at, the Soviet authorities are as he had
                    previously stated (see my 1374, July 18, 5 p.m.) agreeable to the appointment of
                    American Naval Attachés to the Embassy in Moscow. He promised me a formal reply
                    as soon as possible to my note giving the names.</p>
                <p>Lozovski again indicated that permission would not be granted for Major Yeaton to
                    visit the front and said that he had not been able to obtain circulation permit
                    for him.<anchor n="44" xml:id="d858-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d858-f1" n="43" xml:id="d858-f1-content">
                <p>July 25.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d858-f2" n="44" xml:id="d858-f2-content">
                <p>Ambassador Steinhardt was able to report in telegram No. 1417, July 29, 1941,
                    that a circulation card had been issued on July 28 to Major Yeaton
                    (740.00118–European War 1939/446½).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
